1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:06,840
The previous lecture described a triangle
position by vertex weights. Where should

2
00:00:07,160 --> 00:00:13,100
we place a reference marker? The answer
changes with what we want to make equal.

3
00:00:13,750 --> 00:00:18,570
Does a triangle have just one center, and how
can we partition terrain with an inward notch?

4
00:00:18,870 --> 00:00:21,190
We will observe real game tiles and terrain,

5
00:00:21,190 --> 00:00:24,830
then distinguish the questions answered by
centroid, incenter and circumcenter.

6
00:00:25,230 --> 00:00:27,890
Next we will validate polygon boundaries and
convexity,

7
00:00:27,890 --> 00:00:31,230
and examine the limits of a triangle fan.

8
00:00:31,650 --> 00:00:34,850
Finally we will partition a concave example by
ear

9
00:00:34,850 --> 00:00:38,370
clipping and check coverage and thin
triangles.

10
00:00:39,017 --> 00:00:42,717
The complete polygon chapter is split into two
episodes. This one ends after choosing centers

11
00:00:42,857 --> 00:00:49,237
and validating boundaries. The following
episode continues with triangle partitioning.

12
00:00:49,867 --> 00:00:53,467
In Dorfromantik, hexagonal tiles rotate and
enter empty positions.

13
00:00:53,827 --> 00:00:56,287
Observe each tile center and outer edges
together.

14
00:00:58,231 --> 00:01:02,751
Placing a central marker and fitting an
interior circle ask different questions.

15
00:01:04,675 --> 00:01:07,535
The center of a circle through the corners
answers another question.

16
00:01:07,955 --> 00:01:10,235
Shape can change all three answers.

17
00:01:12,079 --> 00:01:15,499
Symmetric regular hexagons make centers appear
coincident;

18
00:01:15,879 --> 00:01:18,619
an arbitrary triangle need not do so.

19
00:01:20,583 --> 00:01:23,403
Connected tiles change the entire land
outline.

20
00:01:23,463 --> 00:01:26,883
Distinguish one tile from the combined region.

21
00:01:28,827 --> 00:01:32,147
We will compare a three-four-five triangle in
our own defined example,

22
00:01:32,687 --> 00:01:36,647
without inferring the game implementation.

23
00:01:37,367 --> 00:01:43,107
Mix three points with equal weights. A weight
of one third per point gives their

24
00:01:43,287 --> 00:01:46,827
coordinate average and connects to the balance
point of a uniform paper triangle.

25
00:01:47,147 --> 00:01:52,067
Equal distances to every side or every vertex
were not imposed.

26
00:01:52,700 --> 00:01:58,360
The centroid is the average of three triangle
vertices and the intersection of the medians.

27
00:01:58,640 --> 00:02:00,880
The incenter is equally distant from the three

28
00:02:01,260 --> 00:02:04,040
sides and centers a circle tangent inside
them.

29
00:02:04,360 --> 00:02:06,860
The circumcenter is equally distant from the

30
00:02:07,120 --> 00:02:09,700
three vertices and centers a circle through
them.

31
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:13,640
Coordinate average, distance to sides,

32
00:02:14,120 --> 00:02:16,120
and distance to vertices: decide what should
be equal first.

33
00:02:16,261 --> 00:02:19,840
Compare the right triangle with vertices
zero-zero,

34
00:02:19,840 --> 00:02:22,680
four-zero and zero-three on the same diagram.

35
00:02:22,700 --> 00:02:25,300
The three centers differ here.

36
00:02:25,300 --> 00:02:28,700
Their shared name does not make their roles
interchangeable.

37
00:02:29,400 --> 00:02:35,020
Add the three vertices and divide by three.
Our centroid is four thirds, one.

38
00:02:35,260 --> 00:02:38,580
A median joins a vertex to the opposite
midpoint;

39
00:02:39,080 --> 00:02:42,160
the centroid lies two thirds along it from the
vertex.

40
00:02:42,600 --> 00:02:48,180
The other medians meet the same point. Each
vertex has barycentric weight one third.

41
00:02:48,460 --> 00:02:52,320
It is also the mass center of a uniform
triangular lamina.

42
00:02:52,380 --> 00:02:55,660
Nonuniform density requires a different
calculation.

43
00:02:55,660 --> 00:03:01,020
For a polygon partition, weight triangle
centroids by area.

44
00:03:01,240 --> 00:03:04,120
Averaging polygon vertices generally gives the
wrong area centroid.

45
00:03:04,300 --> 00:03:06,860
Collinear vertices still have a coordinate
average but no

46
00:03:06,860 --> 00:03:12,180
nonzero-area triangular lamina. Validate the
input separately.

47
00:03:12,800 --> 00:03:16,460
In Tiny Glade, place a building and change its
size and position. Blue marks an

48
00:03:16,700 --> 00:03:25,091
observed reference; choosing one reference
does not replace its complete outline.

49
00:03:26,767 --> 00:03:30,187
In a separate shot, rotate a building and
change its height.

50
00:03:31,608 --> 00:03:34,648
Its outline changes even with a similar center
marker.

51
00:03:36,050 --> 00:03:39,630
This is an observed screen reference, not a
claim about internal game calculations.

52
00:03:40,250 --> 00:03:43,590
In Serious Sam2, observe the walkable surface

53
00:03:43,950 --> 00:03:47,230
and edges while moving between paths and
bridges.

54
00:03:48,946 --> 00:03:55,126
A marker location and clearance from
boundaries are different geometric problems.

55
00:03:57,142 --> 00:04:04,038
For a defined triangular platform, the vertex
average can be one marker reference.

56
00:04:04,318 --> 00:04:13,454
It does not have equal distances to all sides.
Circular clearance needs side distances.

57
00:04:13,454 --> 00:04:15,894
Actual paths can have complex outlines.

58
00:04:16,334 --> 00:04:20,454
Replacing them with one triangle loses
boundaries.

59
00:04:22,250 --> 00:04:25,050
Our next incenter example verifies equal side
distances

60
00:04:25,990 --> 00:04:30,290
numerically rather than claiming the game
collision method.

61
00:04:31,250 --> 00:04:34,590
Drop perpendiculars from an interior point to
two sides. On the angle bisector,

62
00:04:34,850 --> 00:04:39,870
the two right triangles are congruent, giving
equal perpendicular distances.

63
00:04:40,470 --> 00:04:43,610
Join a point equally distant from all three
sides to the vertices. The three small

64
00:04:43,890 --> 00:04:48,950
triangles share the same radius as height.
Their areas, and hence the barycentric area

65
00:04:48,950 --> 00:04:52,410
weights from the previous lecture, are
proportional to their opposite side lengths.

66
00:04:53,333 --> 00:04:56,413
Angle bisectors meet at the incenter.

67
00:04:56,653 --> 00:05:00,333
Weight each vertex by the length of its
opposite side.

68
00:05:00,673 --> 00:05:07,693
The opposite lengths are five, three and four,
with perimeter twelve.

69
00:05:07,973 --> 00:05:10,493
Divide the weighted sum by twelve to obtain
one-one,

70
00:05:10,813 --> 00:05:13,353
at distance one from both coordinate axes.

71
00:05:13,733 --> 00:05:17,353
The hypotenuse is three x plus four y minus
twelve equals zero.

72
00:05:17,553 --> 00:05:20,793
At the incenter, its absolute residual divided
by five is also one.

73
00:05:20,793 --> 00:05:24,013
The inradius is twice area divided by
perimeter:

74
00:05:24,233 --> 00:05:27,313
twice six divided by twelve equals one.

75
00:05:27,493 --> 00:05:29,773
Equivalently divide area by semiperimeter.

76
00:05:30,793 --> 00:05:34,333
Confusing perimeter with semiperimeter
produces half the correct radius.

77
00:05:35,150 --> 00:05:38,150
We have solved equal distances to sides. Now
require equal distances to vertices.

78
00:05:38,390 --> 00:05:44,230
A perpendicular line through the midpoint of
two points consists of

79
00:05:44,650 --> 00:05:49,690
positions equally distant from them. This is
the perpendicular bisector.

80
00:05:50,317 --> 00:05:54,517
Intersect perpendicular bisectors of two sides

81
00:05:54,647 --> 00:05:57,057
to find a center equally distant from the
vertices.

82
00:05:57,277 --> 00:06:00,937
Midpoints two-zero and zero-one point five
give

83
00:06:00,937 --> 00:06:05,537
intersecting bisectors at two-one point five.

84
00:06:05,877 --> 00:06:08,697
Its distance from the origin is two point
five,

85
00:06:08,897 --> 00:06:12,857
equal to the other vertex distances: the
circumradius.

86
00:06:13,077 --> 00:06:15,877
A right triangle places the center at the
hypotenuse midpoint;

87
00:06:15,877 --> 00:06:20,597
an acute triangle places it inside and an
obtuse triangle outside.

88
00:06:21,037 --> 00:06:25,277
The obtuse triangle zero-zero, four-zero,
one-one has circumcenter two-minus one,

89
00:06:25,897 --> 00:06:28,797
outside but still satisfying the condition.

90
00:06:29,337 --> 00:06:32,757
Collinear points do not determine a unique
finite circumcircle;

91
00:06:33,337 --> 00:06:36,437
nearly collinear inputs are numerically
unstable.

92
00:06:37,250 --> 00:06:44,540
A wall stroke creates an open arch. Red
follows the observed wall boundary.

93
00:06:45,930 --> 00:06:50,050
In a separate shot, erase and redraw a wall,
then run a path through it. Watch both the

94
00:06:51,618 --> 00:06:57,947
remaining wall and empty opening: the full
boundary conveys more than one center point.

95
00:06:58,567 --> 00:07:01,767
In Noita, observe protrusions and inward

96
00:07:02,027 --> 00:07:04,767
notches while moving through irregular
terrain.

97
00:07:08,149 --> 00:07:11,889
A polygon records vertices in boundary order.

98
00:07:12,229 --> 00:07:15,489
Reordering the same points changes the shape.

99
00:07:15,769 --> 00:07:24,252
A shortcut across an inward boundary can
incorrectly fill a passage or empty region.

100
00:07:27,935 --> 00:07:33,715
Repeated points and tiny edges can destabilize
direction and area calculations.

101
00:07:33,775 --> 00:07:40,657
We use visible contours to motivate valid
input,

102
00:07:40,657 --> 00:07:43,917
without asserting a triangle mesh
implementation for pixel terrain.

103
00:07:47,700 --> 00:07:51,340
Our next diagram defines a simple six-vertex
concave

104
00:07:51,840 --> 00:07:54,300
polygon and checks its ordered boundary and
missing region.

105
00:07:55,200 --> 00:07:58,420
A single center cannot describe the land
shape. Connect boundary points

106
00:07:58,700 --> 00:08:04,100
in order and close the path to distinguish
retained area from empty space.

107
00:08:04,400 --> 00:08:07,980
We now move from choosing a center to
preserving a boundary.

108
00:08:08,600 --> 00:08:12,560
A polygon boundary closes from its last vertex
to its first;

109
00:08:12,840 --> 00:08:15,600
store vertices in boundary order.

110
00:08:15,780 --> 00:08:22,920
Our L runs through zero-zero, four-zero,
four-one, one-one, one-four, zero-four.

111
00:08:23,160 --> 00:08:28,440
The upper-right missing region is outside;
one-one is the concave vertex.

112
00:08:28,760 --> 00:08:34,540
Check finite coordinates, duplicate points,
zero-length edges and zero-area input first.

113
00:08:34,580 --> 00:08:40,620
For3D vertices, check coplanarity and
intersections of nonadjacent edges as well.

114
00:08:41,120 --> 00:08:44,740
We partition a simple polygon without holes or
self-intersections.

115
00:08:45,180 --> 00:08:48,260
Holes require additional contours and a
suitable algorithm.

116
00:08:49,050 --> 00:08:53,690
A convex region contains the entire segment
between

117
00:08:54,030 --> 00:08:56,370
every two of its points; it has no inward
notch.

118
00:08:56,610 --> 00:08:58,870
A regular hexagon and a triangle are convex:

119
00:08:59,150 --> 00:09:02,450
interior point pairs stay inside when
connected.

120
00:09:02,570 --> 00:09:04,970
In our L, a segment from upper-left to
lower-right

121
00:09:04,970 --> 00:09:08,490
crosses the missing upper-right region.

122
00:09:08,850 --> 00:09:14,570
Its endpoints are inside but part of the
segment is outside, proving concavity.

123
00:09:14,570 --> 00:09:21,010
For a valid simple planar boundary, consistent
turn orientation provides another check.

124
00:09:21,210 --> 00:09:25,130
Do not apply a turn test blindly to a
self-intersecting contour.

125
00:09:25,370 --> 00:09:27,910
Validate order and intersections first.

126
00:09:28,533 --> 00:09:31,553
Tiny Glade changes terrain height.

127
00:09:32,265 --> 00:09:36,345
Different heights cannot automatically be
treated as one planar boundary.

128
00:09:37,217 --> 00:09:43,426
In a separate Noita cave, pass through a
narrow opening and move

129
00:09:43,426 --> 00:09:49,194
toward the right ledge. Red follows visible
land versus empty space.

130
00:09:49,394 --> 00:09:54,463
Bridging a notch could create land outside the
original shape.

131
00:09:55,983 --> 00:10:00,183
Adding Dorfromantik hexagons changes the land
boundary.

132
00:10:00,483 --> 00:10:03,823
Distinguish one tile from the entire connected
region.

133
00:10:06,819 --> 00:10:11,279
An individual tile is convex while its union
can contain inward notches.

134
00:10:14,215 --> 00:10:20,675
Connecting two land positions can cross an
empty tile location.

135
00:10:20,855 --> 00:10:29,531
A union of convex pieces is not necessarily
convex; test its complete boundary separately.

136
00:10:29,711 --> 00:10:38,807
An area with holes needs inner exclusion
contours in addition to the outer boundary.

137
00:10:41,663 --> 00:10:48,363
We will examine whole-shape convexity and why
an angle sum alone cannot distinguish it.

138
00:10:48,983 --> 00:10:53,363
A Dorfromantik tile and a Tiny Glade building
need placement references.

139
00:10:53,763 --> 00:10:56,483
A Serious Sam screen marker helps locate a
target,

140
00:10:56,663 --> 00:11:00,723
while a Noita terrain boundary separates open
space from solid land.

141
00:11:01,243 --> 00:11:04,603
Across these different purposes, one point and
a complete outline carry

142
00:11:05,043 --> 00:11:07,903
different information. Equal center points do
not make two shapes identical.

143
00:11:08,223 --> 00:11:12,403
Choose coordinate averaging or equal distances
to sides before calculating.

144
00:11:12,843 --> 00:11:15,883
These observations do not identify the games’
internal implementation.

145
00:11:16,583 --> 00:11:22,371
In another Noita interval, the character
passes the right ledge.

146
00:11:23,499 --> 00:11:25,019
A point may mark the character, but the red
terrain outline

147
00:11:25,019 --> 00:11:29,119
and the empty gap beside it remain separate
information.

148
00:11:30,567 --> 00:11:34,067
A separate shot descends and returns upward.
Bridging across the

149
00:11:38,013 --> 00:11:43,913
ledge’s notch could incorrectly fill space
that was originally open.

150
00:11:44,533 --> 00:11:50,653
The segment crossing empty space showed that
the L is concave. Turning along its boundary

151
00:11:50,772 --> 00:11:54,973
checks the same property numerically. At its
notch, travel three left and then three

152
00:11:55,173 --> 00:11:59,553
up: the2D cross product is minus nine, the
opposite turn from the other vertices.

153
00:12:00,183 --> 00:12:04,463
Inspect the2D cross sign of consecutive edges.

154
00:12:04,863 --> 00:12:09,683
With counterclockwise winding, convex vertices
turn left.

155
00:12:09,903 --> 00:12:16,043
The L notch turns right, mixing turn signs in
this valid simple example.

156
00:12:16,263 --> 00:12:19,943
Collinear triples have zero cross product.

157
00:12:19,943 --> 00:12:23,103
Define strict convexity or a policy allowing
collinear points.

158
00:12:23,503 --> 00:12:27,403
Every simple polygon has interior-angle sum n
minus

159
00:12:27,403 --> 00:12:31,003
two times180 degrees, whether convex or
concave.

160
00:12:31,263 --> 00:12:37,143
Six vertices give720 degrees. A correct angle
sum alone does not prove convexity.

161
00:12:37,420 --> 00:12:43,663
For acos, reject zero edges and clamp its
input to minus one through one.

162
00:12:43,863 --> 00:12:46,563
The smaller unsigned angle does not identify a
reflex interior angle.

163
00:12:47,183 --> 00:12:53,243
We chose centers by their purpose and found
the notch in an ordered boundary.

164
00:12:53,483 --> 00:12:58,063
A boundary defines the outside, but does not
yet partition the interior.

165
00:12:58,343 --> 00:13:02,123
The next episode divides this L while
preserving its empty region.
